1. Direct physical coercion

These verbs imply physical force or direct bodily compulsion:

force – She forced him to leave.

push – They pushed the door open.

shove – He shoved them into the room.

drag – They dragged her out of the office.

haul – They hauled him to the station.

carry off / seize / grab – The kidnappers seized him.

pin / restrain / tie up – The guard pinned him down.

2. Psychological / social coercion

These verbs imply pressure, threats, or social compulsion rather than physical force:

coerce – They coerced him into signing.

compel – The law compels citizens to pay taxes.

oblige / obligate – The rules oblige you to comply.

pressure – The manager pressured her to stay late.

intimidate / bully / menace – He was intimidated into confessing.

threaten – They threatened him to get the information.

dictate – Circumstances dictated their actions.

command / order – The officer ordered the soldiers to march.

3. Legal / institutional coercion

Verbs that express formal, rule-based compulsion:

mandate – The policy mandates reporting.

enforce – The law enforces attendance.

require – They require all employees to sign in.

instruct – The teacher instructed them to write the essay.

authorize / sanction – The agency authorized him to proceed (implied obligation).

4. Resultative / indirect coercion

These verbs focus on producing an outcome via coercion:

make / have (someone do) – He made her apologize.

bring about – The crisis brought about compliance.

drive – Necessity drove him to act.

push into / steer into – Circumstances pushed them into bankruptcy.

5. Emotional / moral coercion

These verbs rely on guilt, persuasion, or internal pressure:

shame – They shamed him into resigning.

guilt – She guilted him into helping.

pressure / urge – They urged him to take part.
